sketchBlocks.bodyMarkdown:
Validation is easier to reason about when it is a pure decision step.

```js
function validateEvent(input) {
  const fields = [];

  if (!input.title || typeof input.title !== "string") {
    fields.push("title");
  }

  if (!input.category || typeof input.category !== "string") {
    fields.push("category");
  }

  return fields;
}
```

**Step by step:** The example keeps validation in one focused function instead of scattering checks across route handlers. The function receives plain input data, checks each required field against the API contract, and collects the names of fields that are missing or invalid. It returns that result to the route without sending an HTTP response itself. The route can then decide whether to continue with the mutation or return a predictable client error.

The helper does not mutate the collection or send a response. It only reports problems. The route decides whether to reject the request or continue to mutation.

